Z||000012||pontiacs4me||06-15-2001||09:26 PM||||hey bam, I do have pics, check out the first post.  <BR>Cadaver, the molds are made of plaster, and with the humidity here right now, they will take a few days until I feel safe handling them.  They should be ok by tomorrow afternoon.  About the gel coat, I dont have any and I dont know where to get it, so I dont use it.  Im still a novice in fiberglass.  I do have a really good mold release though.  Comes out real smooth.<BR>I just plan to clean the mold release off and spray a coat of primer on there and ship them off.||||reg||1
Z||000013||ArchMagi||06-15-2001||10:21 PM||||Awesome!<P>Id buy them  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||AusFiero||06-16-2001||08:35 AM||||Nice looking scoops Pontiacs4me but from listening to your manufacturing method you are going to run into a whole lot of grief if you don't watch it. <P>A: Gelcoat has plenty of reasons for being there. Not even going to go into the physics of it. Also how thick is the fibreglass laminate you intend to use?<P>B: Plaster Mould? Fibreglass curing creates heat. Plaster reacts to heat. Your mould will not last long and you will possibly end up with warped panels. You also mentioned a good release agent??? The only release agent is a highly polished mould with a good coating of release wax polished to a mirror finish. Yes there is others but we are talking automotive panels here not industrial stuff. Also the wrong mould release agents can pre-release in the mould resulting in shrunk or warped panels.<P>C: Come in primer. What type? A 2 Pack Primer, Acrylic, Enamel, etc etc. The one you use may react to the one people want to use. The panels you show can be reproduced to be identical to the originals without all this work by using a proper mould and gelcoat etc.<P>I am telling you this for your own good. Not to flame you. I just suggest you look a little more into basic fibreglass manufacturing standards before jumping in both feet. <P>Practice a little. Buy a book and learn the basics. Then go for it. Produce a product that you can be proud of.<P>I will probably get flamed for this post but after reading posts like outlaw that jumped in without looking at the big picture. Well he ended up with a whole lot of people very unhappy at him.<P>As I said. Go for it when you do more practice and learn the right way as the right way really isn't that hard once you seen it done.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000015||pontiacs4me||06-16-2001||10:45 AM||||Aus,<BR>The fiberglass laminate is probably about 3/32 to 1/8 of an inch thick.  This mold release is real smooth and real glossy as sprayed.  When I say that the parts need to be cleaned up, I mean trimming the edges, and sanding for final installation.  The parts are going to be nice.  The molds turned out great.  The first set will go on my car, so everyone can see how they are installed, and exactly that they look like all painted up and everything.  And the primer I talk about is a urethane based catalylized primer.  Pretty much universal with paint products.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Wannabe||06-16-2001||02:16 PM||||I'm interested. I like the fact that tey can be put on without too much finish work. I'm stil partial to the Fieroshop hood vent design, but really don't want to get into the  bodywork and painting required to install them, in the short term anyway. PLease keep us posted!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||AusFiero||06-16-2001||08:11 PM||||You still didn't mentioned what your release agent is. There really is proper products to use. Your talking preparation for paint. A fibreglass product, particularly a scoop should be coming out of the mould only needing wet and dry 600 sanding for final paint. And a quick san along the green trimming marks with 40 grade dry. Ready for shipping in a reasonably shiny finish basically. Of course with 2 part moulds you should expect a fine raised line where the join is, even that should not be hardly noticable but. The lack of gelcoat will be your main downfall because as mentioned it is a necessary part of a fibreglass laminate used in the automotive industry. Again I am not going into the actual physics of it because you know why it must be used, right? <P>Do you intend to reinforce all sharp edges with fibreglass rovings? Or just bog up all your airbubbles with filler?<P>I also find it hard to believe that the car they are originally off has the exact same bonnet curve as a Fiero. I really don't think they will fit properly without modification. Properly being a precise fit without trying to stretch them down onto the panels and having no gaps at all around them<P>Your main hurdle will be the GM legal department when they see you are poorly copying one of their products that has had mega dollars poured into production, and they will see eventually. They are far from stupid.<P>A guy over here tried to copy Holdens HSV products. Didn't take long for the full brunt of the GM legal team to do him out of $50,000 and almost have him put in jail.<P>Put again the choice is yours. I am just trying to offer you some advise from years of experience in this industry. Again.<P>Learn how to do the job properly first, then and only then develop your own scoops, not copy GM unless you have lots of money to throw around and a few years where you don't mind just hanging with the boys on A Block.<BR> <A HREF="http://www.v8archie.com" TARGET=_blank>www.v8archie.com</A>  actually has quite some good articles on vultures as he puts em. This is people that copy products already developed and paid for by someone else. <P>But if advice goes over your head....well have a nice time in prison when GM gets you. If the unhappy customers with the newly showing fibreglass texture coming through their paint don't get you first. Thats just one purpose of gelcoat by the way.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000018||pontiacs4me||06-16-2001||10:28 PM||||Ok, thats enough flaming me aus.  Im just trying to make a reasonable priced vent for fellow fiero lovers.  I thought the design was really cool, and the price was totally retarded.  About the GM issue, they arent exact copys, for one the gm one is urethane plastic, mine will be fiberglass.  Mine have a different screen mesh, and will attach slightly differently.  About the fiberglass strands showing through, thats what the primer surfacer is for.  Stop worrying about my business and get on the stage 2 body kit!||||reg||1
Z||000019||AusFiero||06-16-2001||11:18 PM||||I am not flaming you Pontiacs4me...just offering advise on the hard facts of the business world, which is the one you will join soon as you sell something. There is such things as standards and ethics. Your are following neither of them and are gunna get bitten big time if you don't watch yourself. As I said. You can heed advise or ignore it and suffer the consequences.<P>If you want to offer a good product cheap...change the design a bit, add a bit here, remove a bit there so it isn't Pontiacs design and learn how to make them right. <P>Hell I will even offer you advice on the right ways to manufacture something out of fibreglass if you get stuck, you only have to ask. There is others that will probably help too, Ferrari_Momo knows fibreglass from what I have seen and so does Cadavar<BR>also.<P>Advise for someones own good is not a flame but take it as you please as it is no skin off my nose when, not if, you get burnt.<P>Oh by the way. Fibreglass strands aren't stopped showing through by primering and rubbing back, just delayed by about a year depending on the weather conditions and UV rays etc the painted product is exposed too. But you knew that too didn't you? <P>My fibreglass guy is currently resurfacing a motorhome that the owner tried to save money on by not using Gelcoat. 1 year later it shows it and has turned into a major repair, involving vitually a rebody.<P>Making GM's product with a different material and a different mesh and or mounts would not stand up in court. They arent the bits with the patents on them. You would have to physically change the shape, new radiuses on corners, different depth scoops etc. Then it is untouchable by them. More advise I hope you heed as they do have great potential if you do your homework first.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000020||pontiacs4me||06-17-2001||12:23 AM||||AUS,<P>where do I get gelcoat?  Oh yeah and I do know about fiberglass strands coming through, I own a small body shop, and have been doing body work for 4 years.  I attended mcpherson colleges antique auto restoration program also.  I have done alot of fiberglass work, just not any mold building.  I am thinking of changing the design a little, and changing all my posts to say "custom hood vents" rather than GTP vents.  Because they arent gtp vents, they are custom vents, because they are made for fieros, not gtp's.  As a matter of a fact from here on out, they will be titled paragon hood vents.  Monday im making the first set of paragon hood vents.  Ill keep everyone posted as to the "quality".||||reg||1
Z||000021||AusFiero||06-17-2001||01:18 AM||||Glad you are listening Pontiacs..I really wasn't flaming you..just concerned for you. <P>Gelcoat can be bought from most major suppliers of fibreglass. Where are you buying your glass now? <P>If not a major supplier look in your phone book and you should be able to find one in your area. You should be able to buy a good quanitity of matt, resin,  catylist and release waxes etc for a cheap price. One tin of release wax should be good to keep your mould immaculantly polished for about 200 sets of scoops. <P>If no supplier in your area, look for boat builders or any large fibreglass related industry. They will probably be willing to sell you all you will need for a fair price as they buy in bulk for a good rate. Or if they won't sell you any they should be able to put you onto the local supplier.<P>Ask about tooling gel as well because that is what you will need to make a good mould that will last you for years. You wont need a lot of tooling gel for moulds that size. Tooling gel isn't an absolute necessity but it does stand up better for moulds than standard gelcoat. If you want the mould to last for a long period it is probably the way to go. Don't mind working on the mould to get it pristine again every now and then, normal gelcoat will do.<P>Glad to hear your changing the shape because man them lawyer types are visious with this stuff and I know it does look sweet on a Fiero. <P>If you want I can send you a list of exactly what to ask for at the material suppliers rather than going in blind. Also if your stuck just contact me and I will step you through the process of making a good mould and how to manufacture a glass smooth version that if you made in black for instance could be a direct bolt on without painting, straight from the mould. Better than GMs plastic and probably cheaper and less time consuming for yourself to make if orders come for lots of them. <P>I would rather share this info around than see you learn the hard way because hell I have learn't plenty of what not to do's over the years I tell you, the hard way.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">   <P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">    <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?<P>PS Paragon sounds good too, still has a very Pontiacish ring to it, much better than stage 2 bodykit, god I need a name for that. <p>[This message has been edited by AusFiero (edited 06-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||cadaver||06-17-2001||03:20 AM||||Thank you Aus. I have done a  bit of 'glass work. Lol I have my own chopper gun and a gel coat gun.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Smoooooth GT||06-17-2001||04:04 AM||||I've done a lil' fiberglassing too... But No mold's or anything like that...  Steve<P>------------------<BR>Future add's.. 2" drop, 17" wheel's, finish the turbo T-3watercooled project, ViperYellow w/blk racin' stripes.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/new_sig_2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||AusFiero||06-17-2001||06:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadaver:<BR><B>Thank you Aus. I have done a  bit of 'glass work. Lol I have my own chopper gun and a gel coat gun.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>LOL..well that rates as a bit I suppose. Its not just a workshop ornament hey  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||pontiacs4me||06-17-2001||11:24 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by AusFiero:<BR>[B]<P>Gelcoat can be bought from most major suppliers of fibreglass. Where are you buying your glass now? <P><BR>I get my glass from a auto body supply store.  Same for my resin.  I think I may know who sells gel coat.  <BR>Do you have to have a special gun for gelcoat?  I have 3 spray guns, 2 hvlp and 1 siphon guns.  1 sata jet nr92, and accuspray issac, and a old devilbiss cup gun.  Will any of these work?  What is the consistency of gelcoat, is it thick, runny  etc?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000026||AusFiero||06-17-2001||11:45 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by pontiacs4me:<BR><B><BR>Do you have to have a special gun for gelcoat What is the consistency of gelcoat, is it thick, runny  etc?  Thanks.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Gelcoat is reasonably thick. For scoops the size you are doing it would be better to brush it on. Not worth cleaning a gun as you would already be finished with a brush. The gelcoat supplier will advise you of the ideal thickness and most even sell a cheap plastic thickness guide to check it. But if you work in autopaint already you will know just by the consistancy and coverage that you got it right. An even coat with no translucent areas applied in one coat does the trick. <P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000027||cadaver||06-17-2001||11:49 AM||||Those will not work with gelcoat. But you dont need a gun for it. You can brush it in. You can make it thicker ot thinner depending on what you add to the gel coat. Try it straight out of the can (kicked ofcourse) and see how it works for you. I have never run glass in a plaster mold so there are things I cant say. But your mold will start to show signs of wear very quickly. Little pieces of the mold face will start chipping off leaving bumps on your parts.||||reg||1
Z||000028||Songman||06-17-2001||02:22 PM||||I hate to sound stupid, but I don't know anything about any of this... what do you normally make the molds out of?<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||5
Z||000029||cadaver||06-17-2001||06:54 PM||||Well that all depends on what the material you are going to cast or lay up is. And how complex the part is. For this application I would probably make them out of fiberglass. Nice and thick so I know they wont warp.||||reg||1
Z||000030||rockcrawl||06-17-2001||07:30 PM|||| Definately, definately ditch the plaster molds! Get some tooling gelcoat and make a set of profesional molds that will last a long time. <BR> You need to use gelcoat or like Aus said, the glass will show right through the paint when it sits in the sun.<BR> Don't waste your money on a dump gun. I only use mine when I am doing several larger parts at the same time. It's not worth it for small molds.<BR> I buy materials from US Composites. <A HREF="http://www.uscomposites.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.uscomposites.com/</A>  They have the best prices I can find on the web, and they always ship quickly. Also visit <A HREF="http://www.fiberglast.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiberglast.com/</A>  and read some of their online brochures on mold building and part making. If you have originals to use as plugs, your reproductions should come out of the mold with the exact same perfect finish. But then there's that whole legal issue! <BR> ||||reg||7
Z||000031||fierofrek||06-17-2001||08:12 PM||||Is there a web site someone knows of that will give you a step by step walk through of making moulds, and then producing parts from the moulds? or maybe a "beginners mould page"?<P>PR not producing for sales, but for ones own car. I have been trying to make moulds for a side scoop/rocker panel, and while my mould looks real good, when I apply fiber glass to the mould, it kinda craps out.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by fierofrek (edited 06-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000032||rockcrawl||06-17-2001||09:36 PM||||fierofrek, click on <A HREF="http://www.fiberglast.com/fiberglass_repair.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiberglast.com/fiberglass_repair.htm</A>  and start reading. It doesn't get much better than that.||||reg||1
Z||000033||AusFiero||06-18-2001||12:45 AM||||Yep Cadavar already said it. Fibreglass moulds. Mice and thick to resist warpage.<P>A good laminate for a mould is as follows. Tooling gel to manufacturers recommended thickness, let dry.<P>1 layer of fibreglass tissue and cut lengths of rovings to fill any sharp edges and to reinforce them. Make sure you roll out all air bubles no matter how small. Work from the middle out. Use the recommended amounts of catylist always otherwise you could cause pre-releasing if it goes off to quick. Let it go off.<P>Next give the now cured surface a light, careful, but quick sand with 40 grit paper. This is only to remove any dags that may cause air bubbles in the next part of the job.<P>Apply 2 layers of 450 gram mat. Roll out the air again and repeat the stages of sanding etc.<P>Now you have a good shaped but not yet strong enough mould. Now do another laminate  of 3 layers of 450 gram mat, repeat processes to sanding.<P>Next cut some wooden legs and reinforcement panels to keep everything nice and stiff and square. <BR>These don't have to be real hightech, just cut a reasonable fit to reinforce an flexing that may occur. Glue them on with a glue gun or even plastesine, it is only to locate them for glassing. Then glass over them and the whole mould with 2 more layers of 450 gram mat. <P>Thats it for laminatiing, pop it off after curing and cut the trimming flanges down to size.<P>Have not covered mould release agents, trimming flanges or making the plug here...only the laminate.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/fiero5.gif">   <BR><A HREF="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero" TARGET=_blank>My Fiero</A><BR>Need a Subwoofer Console or Speaker Door Pods?||||reg||1
Z||000034||fierofrek||06-18-2001||04:37 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by rockcrawl:<BR><B>fierofrek, click on <A HREF="http://www.fiberglast.com/fiberglass_repair.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiberglast.com/fiberglass_repair.htm</A>  and start reading. It doesn't get much better than that.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>cool, thanks!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>ok, been reading at this site for a couple hours, and am still confused with one thing, is one supposed to use a "releasing agent", or a "gel coat"?<BR>Thx for your answers I know I seem stupid, but never done this kind of work before.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by fierofrek (edited 06-18-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000035||AusFiero||06-18-2001||06:35 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierofrek:<BR><B>  cool, thanks!    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>ok, been reading at this site for a couple hours, and am still confused with one thing, is one supposed to use a "releasing agent", or a "gel coat"?<BR>Thx for your answers I know I seem stupid, but never done this kind of work before.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by fierofrek (edited 06-18-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Both. A release agent is basically to stop your gelcoat from sticking to your mould or to the product you are trying to make a mould off. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000036||FieroTig||06-18-2001||06:26 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.nacogames.com/fiero/adams/gtpvents4.jpg"> <P>i don't know if i like them or not, but for those of you that where trying to imagine them at the same color as the car, here you go....<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://209.125.196.179/fiero/adams/FieroTig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000037||cadaver||06-18-2001||10:10 PM||||This is what I think I would do with them. Dont take this badly.<BR>I think they look pretty good. But I would square the fronts to more match the line of the front of the hood. Fiero's are not real sharp and pointy.<BR> I would do a clay pour/press and do a resculpt them make another set of originals. That would also get away from the original design with out chaning the look too much. But thats just me.||||reg||1
